   "citation": "Garg M, Choudhuri S, Parrish C, Hawkins S, Lachmann H, Clayton L, Richardson J, Chan YLT, Wechalekar A. Br J Haematol. 2026;208(6):1931–1938.",
   "pdfUrl": "/assets/aistudio/Br_J_Haematol_-_2026_-_Garg_-_Early_diagnosis_of_AL_amyloidosis_in_haematology_cardiology_neurology_renal_and_general_b50657c2.pdf",
   "onlineUrl": "https://doi.org/10.1111/bjh.70472",
   "overview": "AL amyloidosis is caused by a plasma cell clone, usually with less than 10% marrow plasma cells (a 'small dangerous clone'), that makes misfolded light chains. These deposit as amyloid fibrils and damage organs. Survival depends on how much organ damage is present at diagnosis, and most patients are still diagnosed late. This guideline is aimed mainly at non-haematologists. It sets out the biomarkers and red flags that should raise suspicion in MGUS, cardiology, renal and neurology clinics, and says when to refer urgently. It is written to prompt suspicion and referral, not to guide treatment.",
